  "detail": null,
  "summary": "The Monday 10/26 St. Brigid CT list is ready. Four studies were booked and each has an order linked to its appointment. Three other appointments that day (pt-0048, pt-0012, pt-0050) were cancelled, so I left them alone. Four new items came into your inbox while I worked, and three of them changed my earlier decisions. Every patient got instructions, and anyone who had already been messaged got a correction.\n\n1. **Mohr (pt-0047), 08:00, CT abdomen/pelvis with contrast.** He has type 2 diabetes managed with diet only, and no allergies.\n   - His last creatinine (9/24) was more than 30 days before the scan, so I ordered a new one (sr-t0007).\n   - A creatinine from Northside Urgent Care then arrived: 1.52 on 10/22, up from 0.94 in September. eGFR is about 64. That counts as a recent result, and it doesn't meet the policy's definition of acute kidney injury, so no IV fluids are needed and the scan goes ahead as booked.\n   - **Decision for you (task-t0027):** the new creatinine order is no longer required, but I can't cancel lab orders with my tools. Please cancel it or keep it as a recheck, and review why his creatinine rose.\n   - The patient was told no more pre-scan blood work is needed.\n\n2. **Ferreira (pt-0015), 09:00, CT abdomen/pelvis with contrast.** The allergy list says \"anaphylaxis\" (his own report). The 2019 radiology report from the day itself grades the reaction as moderate (hives, eyelid swelling), and that grade is what the policy uses.\n   - Premedication ordered: prednisone 50 mg at 20:00 on 10/25 and at 02:00 and 08:00 on 10/26, plus diphenhydramine 50 mg at 08:00. He needs a driver.\n   - The lab then corrected his 10/14 creatinine from 0.98 to 2.8; the original value came from another patient's sample. That puts his eGFR at about 24, so I added 500 mL of IV saline starting at 08:00.\n   - I sent a corrected message in Spanish and English: arrive at 08:00 for the fluids, and my earlier statement that his kidney test was normal is withdrawn.\n   - Task-t0028 asks you to follow up on his newly found reduced kidney function.\n\n3. **Schuster (pt-0049), 13:00, CT abdomen/pelvis with contrast.** She has type 2 diabetes on metformin, and had acute kidney injury in the ED on 10/4 (creatinine 1.52, still 1.24 at discharge; no result since).\n   - I ordered a creatinine to be drawn before the scan and 500 mL of IV saline at 12:00.\n   - I put metformin on hold: no dose on the scan day or for 48 hours after, and no restart until kidney function is rechecked and normal. Task-t0015, due 10/28, is your reminder to recheck and restart.\n   - A faxed 2022 Lakeshore report then showed a moderate reaction to iopamidol contrast. I added premedication: prednisone 50 mg at 00:00, 06:00 and 12:00 on 10/26, plus diphenhydramine 50 mg at 12:00. She needs a driver, and she was sent updated instructions.\n   - **Still needs doing:** iopamidol isn't in the catalog, so I couldn't add it to her allergy list. Someone needs to add it by hand.\n\n4. **Beahan (pt-0046), 14:00, CT abdomen/pelvis without contrast (stone protocol).** No contrast, so no prep was needed and she was told nothing special is required.\n   - A faxed record showed a mild reaction to iohexol contrast in 2024. I added it to her allergy list. It doesn't change Monday's non-contrast scan.\n\nEach patient's chart has a note citing RBC-RAD-07, plus an addendum explaining each change. I closed the four new inbox items. Your older inbox items for other patients are still open.",
